'''Achatina''' (ak-a-tī′nä̤), ''n.'' [NL., < L. ''achates'', agate: see ''agate''.] A genus of land-snails, of the family ''Helicidæ''. {{smaller|It is typified by the large agate-shells of Africa, and is distinguished by an intorted and abruptly truncate columella. The species of this genus, which comprises some of the largest terrestrial mollusks, live chiefly near water about trees; they are mostly African. The small species formerly referred to ''Achatina'' are little related to the genus. ''Lamarck'', 1799. Also ''Achatium'' (''Link'', 1807) and ''Agathina'' (''Deshayes'').}}

